{
  "id": "unique-paths-ii",
  "questionId": "unique-paths-ii",
  "slug": "unique-paths-ii",
  "title": "Unique Paths II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Dp 2d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming",
    "grid",
    "combinatorics"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "this is unique paths, but now some cells are simply unreachable - what should dp[i][j] be for an obstacle cell?",
    "define dp[i][j] as the number of ways to reach (i, j); if (i, j) is an obstacle, dp[i][j] = 0 no matter what",
    "otherwise dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1], same as the obstacle-free version, treating out-of-bounds neighbors as 0",
    "the first row and first column aren't automatically all 1s anymore - an obstacle anywhere in them blocks every cell after it in that row/column",
    "handle the very first cell as a special case: dp[0][0] = 1 unless it's itself an obstacle, in which case the whole answer is 0"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "assuming the first row and column are always filled with 1s, forgetting an obstacle can block the rest of that row or column",
    "not checking if the starting cell itself is an obstacle before initializing dp[0][0] = 1",
    "treating the obstacle value 1 as a path count instead of a marker, and accidentally adding it into the sum"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A robot starts in the top-left corner of a grid `obstacleGrid` and wants to reach the bottom-right corner, moving only right or down at each step. Some cells contain obstacles marked with a 1, and the robot cannot step on them; free cells are marked with a 0.\n\n**Return the number of distinct paths from the top-left to the bottom-right corner.**\n\nIf the start or the destination cell itself is an obstacle, there are zero valid paths.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= rows <= 100",
      "1 <= cols <= 100",
      "obstacleGrid[i][j] is 0 or 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the start cell is an obstacle",
      "the destination cell is an obstacle",
      "a single row or single column grid with an obstacle blocking the only path",
      "a grid with no obstacles at all"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "obstacleGrid = [[0,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,0]]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "The obstacle in the middle blocks the straight-through routes, leaving exactly 2 paths around it."
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "obstacleGrid = [[0,1],[0,0]]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": ""
      },
      {
